{:edb/committed true :edb/basis-t 1978 :edb/replayed false :edb/tx-hash "f88a49052e2cb200973f8c8ac9230931d3dc19599fde04977f997a6580b10151" :edb/db-before-t 1977 :edb/db-after-t 1978 :edb/tx-data [[13194139535290 50 #inst "2026-10-08T21:23:28.341Z" 13194139535290 true] [17592186179408 1100 "Consider the circle $S$ and vector field $\\mathbf F$ shown below. Let $C$ be any path where $S$ is traversed once in the counterclockwise direction.\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\n1. The flux of $\\mathbf F$ across $C$ is negative\n2. The flux of $\\mathbf F$ across $C$ is zero\n3. The flux of $\\mathbf F$ across $-C$ is positive\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/3715/Source/Images/0fc4f6ac431f4a118406f367a2049f25.png)" 13194139535290 false] [17592186179408 1100 "Consider the circle $S$ and vector field $\\mathbf F$ shown below. Let $C$ be any path where $S$ is traversed once in the counterclockwise direction. \n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\n- The flux of $\\mathbf F$ across $C$ is negative\n\n- The flux of $\\mathbf F$ across $C$ is zero\n\n- The flux of $\\mathbf F$ across $-C$ is positive\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14061989/assets/7e7fbe6aaf1310b186344a3cf5a127075f11a86b51db2e687ddc7035d5d890f8.png)" 13194139535290 true] [17592186179408 1145 "If $\\mathbf{F}$ is a vector field and $C$ is a piecewise-smooth, simple, closed curve, then the flux of $\\mathbf F$ across $C$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\oint\\limits_C \\mathbf F \\cdot \\mathbf{n} \\: \\textrm{d}s,\n$$\n\nwhere $\\mathbf{n}$ denotes the outward-pointing unit normal vector to $C.$\n\nTo understand the flux intuitively, we imagine that $\\mathbf F$ represents a stream of flowing water. The flux of $\\mathbf F$ across $C$ represents the net flow of water escaping the region bounded by $C\\mathbin:$ \n\n- If more water flows out through $C$ than in through $C$ (i.e., there is a net flow *out* through $C$), the flux is *positive*.\n\n- If more water flows in through $C$ than out through $C$ (i.e., there is a net flow *in* through $C$), the flux is *negative*.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14061989/assets/a72dc9866562a42a49590fd169424307675b2c14b36833bd729026f46b677eec.png)\n\n- If the net flow through $C$ is zero, the flux equals zero.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14061989/assets/3a6ac8656b2e0b5cc482a16eb3a4fab57cb681c9a40cf634da47f9a4323d9fdf.png)\n\nThe outward-pointing unit normal vector tells us which direction is \"out.\"\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each statement.\n\n- Statement I is false, while statement II is true. From the diagram below we see that $\\mathbf F$ acts perpendicular to $\\mathbf n$ at every point on $C.$ Therefore, there is no net flow of $\\mathbf F$ through $C.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14061989/assets/4a3d9864bb6bada1fd218b299427bc5bb4ffcbcccb1a504fff77416d5d292151.png)\n\nSo, the flux of $\\mathbf F$ across $C$ is zero, and we must have\n\n$$\n\\oint\\limits_C \\mathbf F \\cdot \\mathbf{n} \\: \\textrm{d}s = 0.\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. When measuring flux through a closed curve, the orientation of the curve does not matter because the outward-pointing unit normal vector is the same at each point on $C$ in either case.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14061989/assets/9310d17389f61de2984c0e23cfe1060e77d4a87a140d2f1c892bdbab9b99629b.png)\n\nSo, the flux of $\\mathbf F$ across $-C$ is zero, and we must have\n\n$$\n\\oint\\limits_{-C} \\mathbf F \\cdot \\mathbf{n} \\: \\textrm{d}s = 0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\"" 13194139535290 true] [17592186179408 1145 "If $\\mathbf{F}$ is a vector field and $C$ is a piecewise-smooth, simple, closed curve, then the flux of $\\mathbf F$ across $C$ is given by\n$\\oint\\limits_C \\mathbf F \\cdot \\mathbf{n} \\: \\textrm{d}s$,\nwhere $\\mathbf{n}$ denotes the outward-pointing unit normal vector to $C$.\n\nTo understand the flux intuitively, we imagine that $\\mathbf F$ represents a stream of flowing water. The flux of $\\mathbf F$ across $C$ represents the net flow of water escaping the region bounded by $C\\mathbin$:\n\n- If more water flows out through $C$ than in through $C$ (i.e., there is a net flow *out* through $C$), the flux is *positive*.\n- If more water flows in through $C$ than out through $C$ (i.e., there is a net flow *in* through $C$), the flux is *negative*.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/3715/Source/Images/ceedec3a772973eda45dc8e75ea8a1b4.png)\n\n- If the net flow through $C$ is zero, the flux equals zero.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/3715/Source/Images/7d3a45be0fad34026a756e0685ef72ed.png)\n\nThe outward-pointing unit normal vector tells us which direction is \"out.\"\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each statement.\n\n- Statement I is false, while statement II is true. From the diagram below we see that $\\mathbf F$ acts perpendicular to $\\mathbf n$ at every point on $C$. Therefore, there is no net flow of $\\mathbf F$ through $C$.![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/3715/Source/Images/f3f7b1aa12b9af600ca4107f8ba7963e.png)\nSo, the flux of $\\mathbf F$ across $C$ is zero, and we must have\n$\\oint\\limits_C \\mathbf F \\cdot \\mathbf{n} \\: \\textrm{d}s = 0$.\n- Statement III is false. When measuring flux through a closed curve, the orientation of the curve does not matter because the outward-pointing unit normal vector is the same at each point on $C$ in either case.![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/3715/Source/Images/f7f6796435787eeb8416a755b211bebc.png)\nSo, the flux of $\\mathbf F$ across $-C$ is zero, and we must have\n$\\oint\\limits_{-C} \\mathbf F \\cdot \\mathbf{n} \\: \\textrm{d}s = 0$.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\"" 13194139535290 false] [17592186179409 1100 "Consider the vector field $\\mathbf F(x,y) = 8y\\,\\mathbf i + 4x\\,\\mathbf j$ and the circle $C$ given by $x^2+y^2 = 4$. If the flux of $\\mathbf F$ across $C$ is given by\n\n$AAAAAAA$\n\nwhat is the missing expression?\n\n*Hint: The outward-pointing unit normal vector to $C$ is $\\mathbf n = \\dfrac x2\\,\\mathbf i + \\dfrac y2\\,\\mathbf j$.*" 13194139535290 false] [17592186179409 1100 "Consider the vector field $\\mathbf F(x,y) = 8y\\,\\mathbf i + 4x\\,\\mathbf j$ and the circle $C$ given by $x^2+y^2 = 4.$ If the flux of $\\mathbf F$ across $C$ is given by  \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14061989/assets/707f56c886072458eefdd7bcee2328992caa214b4f4954ba59c92bc972b66409.png)\n\nwhat is the missing expression?\n\n*Hint: The outward-pointing unit normal vector to $C$ is $\\mathbf n = \\dfrac x2\\,\\mathbf i + \\dfrac y2\\,\\mathbf j.$*" 13194139535290 true] [17592186179409 1145 "If $\\mathbf{F}$ is a vector field and $C$ is a piecewise-smooth, simple, closed curve, then the flux of $\\mathbf F$ across $C$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\oint\\limits_C \\mathbf F \\cdot \\mathbf{n} \\: \\textrm{d}s,\n$$\n\nwhere $\\mathbf{n}$ denotes the outward-pointing unit normal vector to $C.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14061989/assets/ff84680c55f5349431cf3d70066eeaba2c680569faa3b2210e5d14ef63ccd99e.png)\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s & ={∮}_{C}(8y\\,i+4x\\,j)\\cdot (\\frac{x}{2}i+\\frac{y}{2}\\,j)\\text{d}s \\\\  & ={∮}_{C}4xy+2xy\\,\\text{d}s \\\\  & ={∮}_{C}6xy\\,\\text{d}s.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the missing expression is $6xy.$" 13194139535290 true] [17592186179409 1145 "If $\\mathbf{F}$ is a vector field and $C$ is a piecewise-smooth, simple, closed curve, then the flux of $\\mathbf F$ across $C$ is given by\n$\\oint\\limits_C \\mathbf F \\cdot \\mathbf{n} \\: \\textrm{d}s$,\nwhere $\\mathbf{n}$ denotes the outward-pointing unit normal vector to $C$.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/3715/Source/Images/abb3c6599e722f86db8e3d48613846f1.png)\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n∮_(C)F \\cdot nds &= ∮_(C)(8yi + 4xj) \\cdot (\\frac{x}{2}i + \\frac{y}{2}j)ds \\\\\n&= ∮_(C)4xy + 2xyds \\\\\n&= ∮_(C)6xyds\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the missing expression is $6xy$." 13194139535290 false] [17592186179410 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14061989/assets/05c91d681a0c84bbe9d8b6cab9bd8cdf1dca8bf88c851b8c7a7045f73ff9844c.png)\n\nConsider the vector field $\\mathbf F = 2x\\,\\mathbf i + 2y\\,\\mathbf j$ and a square $C$ with side length $2$ centered at $O,$ as shown below. Calculate the flux of $\\mathbf F$ across $C.$\n\n*Hint: You may use the fact that, due to symmetry, the flux is equal across all four sides of the square.*" 13194139535290 true] [17592186179410 1100 "Consider the vector field $\\mathbf F = 2x\\,\\mathbf i + 2y\\,\\mathbf j$ and a square $C$ with side length $2$ centered at $O$, as shown below. Calculate the flux of $\\mathbf F$ across $C$.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/3715/Source/Images/4ba14e820f544a5113eaea401b1ac37f.png)\n\n*Hint: You may use the fact that, due to symmetry, the flux is equal across all four sides of the square.*" 13194139535290 false] [17592186179410 1145 "If $\\mathbf{F}$ is a vector field and $C$ is a piecewise-smooth, simple, closed curve, then the flux of $\\mathbf F$ across $C$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\oint\\limits_C \\mathbf F \\cdot \\mathbf{n} \\: \\textrm{d}s,\n$$\n\nwhere $\\mathbf{n}$ denotes the outward-pointing unit normal vector to $C.$ In this case, we have\n\n$$\n\\oint\\limits_C \\mathbf F \\cdot \\mathbf{n} \\: \\textrm{d}s\n=\n\\int\\limits_{C_1} \\mathbf F \\cdot \\mathbf{n}_1 \\: \\textrm{d}s\n+\n\\int\\limits_{C_2} \\mathbf F \\cdot \\mathbf{n}_2 \\: \\textrm{d}s\n+\n\\int\\limits_{C_3} \\mathbf F \\cdot \\mathbf{n}_3 \\: \\textrm{d}s\n+\n\\int\\limits_{C_4} \\mathbf F \\cdot \\mathbf{n}_4 \\: \\textrm{d}s,\n$$\n\nwhere the sides $C_i$ and unit vectors $\\mathbf n_i$ are shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14061989/assets/1de4c4c55784b4a84a0493a4cc65082c430cadcc39c3d808001b2fd27665e968.png)\n\nFirst, consider the side $C_1.$ We have that $\\mathbf n_1 = \\mathbf i,$ and therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot {n}_{1}\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{{C}_{1}}(2x\\,i+2y\\,j)\\cdot i\\,\\text{d}s \\\\  & =2{\\int }_{{C}_{1}}x\\,\\text{d}s.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is a line integral with respect to arc length, which we can evaluate in the usual way. Parameterizing the side $C_1,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\mathbf r(t) = \\mathbf i + t\\,\\mathbf j, \\quad \\Rightarrow\\quad \\mathbf r'(t) = \\mathbf j, \\qquad t\\in [-1,1].\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{\\int }_{{C}_{1}}x\\,\\text{d}s & =2{\\int }_{-1}^{1}1\\cdot ||{r}^{′}(t)||\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2{\\int }_{-1}^{1}\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2(1-(-1)) \\\\  & =2\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBy symmetry, the flux integrals over the sides $C_2, C_3,$ and $C_4$ must be the same. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\oint\\limits_C \\mathbf F \\cdot \\mathbf{n} \\: \\textrm{d}s = 4\\cdot 4 = 16.\n$$" 13194139535290 true] [17592186179410 1145 "If $\\mathbf{F}$ is a vector field and $C$ is a piecewise-smooth, simple, closed curve, then the flux of $\\mathbf F$ across $C$ is given by\n$\\oint\\limits_C \\mathbf F \\cdot \\mathbf{n} \\: \\textrm{d}s$,\nwhere $\\mathbf{n}$ denotes the outward-pointing unit normal vector to $C$. In this case, we have\n\n$$\n\\oint\\limits_C \\mathbf F \\cdot \\mathbf{n} \\: \\textrm{d}s = \\int\\limits_{C_1} \\mathbf F \\cdot \\mathbf{n}_1 \\: \\textrm{d}s + \\int\\limits_{C_2} \\mathbf F \\cdot \\mathbf{n}_2 \\: \\textrm{d}s + \\int\\limits_{C_3} \\mathbf F \\cdot \\mathbf{n}_3 \\: \\textrm{d}s + \\int\\limits_{C_4} \\mathbf F \\cdot \\mathbf{n}_4 \\: \\textrm{d}s\n$$\n\nwhere the sides $C_i$ and unit vectors $\\mathbf n_i$ are shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/3715/Source/Images/16258660947a1a027a68b4d411919586.png)\n\nFirst, consider the side $C_1$. We have that\n\n$$\n\\mathbf n_1 = \\mathbf i\n$$\n\nand therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n∫_{C_{1}}F \\cdot n_{1}ds &= ∫_{C_{1}}(2xi + 2yj) \\cdot ids \\\\\n&= 2∫_{C_{1}}xds\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is a line integral with respect to arc length, which we can evaluate in the usual way. Parameterizing the side $C_1$, we have\n\n$$\n\\mathbf r(t) = \\mathbf i + t\\,\\mathbf j, \\quad \\Rightarrow\\quad \\mathbf r'(t) = \\mathbf j, \\qquad t\\in [-1,1]\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n2∫_{C_{1}}xds &= 2∫_{-1}^{1}1 \\cdot \\mid \\mid r^{′}(t) \\mid \\mid dt \\\\\n&= 2∫_{-1}^{1}dt \\\\\n&= 2(1 - (-1)) \\\\\n&= 2 \\cdot 2 \\\\\n&= 4\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBy symmetry, the flux integrals over the sides $C_2, C_3$, and $C_4$ must be the same. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\oint\\limits_C \\mathbf F \\cdot \\mathbf{n} \\: \\textrm{d}s = 4\\cdot 4 = 16\n$$" 13194139535290 false] [17592186179411 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14061989/assets/59b5f1676f036f80e6aa2e9e331327ed9ec28e88bacda52a91ee0865736e9ce3.png)\n\nConsider the vector field $\\mathbf F = 5x\\,\\mathbf i - 3y\\,\\mathbf j$ and a rectangle $C$ with base $4$ and height $2$ centered at $O,$ as shown above. Calculate the flux of $\\mathbf F$ across $C.$ \n\n*Hint: You may use the fact that, due to symmetry, the flux is equal across the vertical sides, and similarly for the horizontal sides.*" 13194139535290 true] [17592186179411 1100 "Consider the vector field $\\mathbf F = 5x\\,\\mathbf i - 3y\\,\\mathbf j$ and a rectangle $C$ with base $4$ and height $2$ centered at $O$, as shown above. Calculate the flux of $\\mathbf F$ across $C$.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/3715/Source/Images/14fbee42939abe16d9fd3f09b62b7242.png)\n\n*Hint: You may use the fact that, due to symmetry, the flux is equal across the vertical sides, and similarly for the horizontal sides.*" 13194139535290 false] [17592186179411 1145 "If $\\mathbf{F}$ is a vector field and $C$ is a piecewise-smooth, simple, closed curve, then the flux of $\\mathbf F$ across $C$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\oint\\limits_C \\mathbf F \\cdot \\mathbf{n} \\: \\textrm{d}s,\n$$\n\nwhere $\\mathbf{n}$ denotes the outward-pointing unit normal vector to $C.$ In this case, we have\n\n$$\n\\oint\\limits_C \\mathbf F \\cdot \\mathbf{n} \\: \\textrm{d}s\n=\n\\int\\limits_{C_1} \\mathbf F \\cdot \\mathbf{n}_1 \\: \\textrm{d}s\n+\n\\int\\limits_{C_2} \\mathbf F \\cdot \\mathbf{n}_2 \\: \\textrm{d}s\n+\n\\int\\limits_{C_3} \\mathbf F \\cdot \\mathbf{n}_3 \\: \\textrm{d}s\n+\n\\int\\limits_{C_4} \\mathbf F \\cdot \\mathbf{n}_4 \\: \\textrm{d}s,\n$$\n\nwhere the sides $C_i$ and unit vectors $\\mathbf n_i$ are shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14061989/assets/793755cda5243dbb427d8722c21ab150a4821c3957f0ccd0ef0fedbbd576e924.png)\n\nWe compute this integral in two stages. \n\n- First, consider the side $C_1.$ We have that $\\mathbf n_1 = \\mathbf i,$ and therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot {n}_{1}\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{{C}_{1}}(5x\\,i-3y\\,j)\\cdot i\\,\\text{d}s \\\\  & =5{\\int }_{{C}_{1}}x\\,\\text{d}s.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is a line integral with respect to arc length, which we can evaluate in the usual way. Parameterizing the side $C_1,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\mathbf r(t) = 2\\mathbf i + t\\,\\mathbf j, \\quad \\Rightarrow\\quad \\mathbf r'(t) = \\mathbf j, \\qquad t\\in [-1,1].\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5{\\int }_{{C}_{1}}x\\,\\text{d}s & =5{\\int }_{-1}^{1}2\\cdot ||{r}^{′}(t)||\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =10{\\int }_{-1}^{1}\\text{d}t \\\\  & =10\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =20.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBy symmetry, the flux integral across the side $C_3$ must be the same. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\int\\limits_{C_1} \\mathbf F \\cdot \\mathbf{n}_1 \\: \\textrm{d}s = \\int\\limits_{C_3} \\mathbf F \\cdot \\mathbf{n}_3 \\: \\textrm{d}s = 20.\n$$\n\n- Next, we consider the side $C_2.$ We have that $\\mathbf n_2 = \\mathbf j,$ and therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot {n}_{2}\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{{C}_{2}}(5x\\,i-3y\\,j)\\cdot j\\,\\text{d}s \\\\  & =-3{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}y\\,\\text{d}s.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is a line integral with respect to arc length, which we can evaluate in the usual way. Parameterizing the side $C_2,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\mathbf r(t) = t\\,\\mathbf i + \\mathbf j, \\quad \\Rightarrow\\quad \\mathbf r'(t) = \\mathbf i, \\qquad t\\in [-2,2].\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-3{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}y\\,\\text{d}s & =-3{\\int }_{-2}^{2}1\\cdot ||{r}^{′}(t)||\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =-3{\\int }_{-2}^{2}\\text{d}t \\\\  & =(-3)\\cdot 4 \\\\  & =-12.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBy symmetry, the flux integral across the side $C_4$ must be the same. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\int\\limits_{C_2} \\mathbf F \\cdot \\mathbf{n}_2 \\: \\textrm{d}s = \\int\\limits_{C_4} \\mathbf F \\cdot \\mathbf{n}_4 \\: \\textrm{d}s = -12.\n$$\n\nFinally, then\n\n$$\n\\oint\\limits_{C}\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathbf n\\,\\textrm d s= 2 \\cdot 20 + 2 \\cdot (-12) = 16.\n$$" 13194139535290 true] [17592186179411 1145 "If $\\mathbf{F}$ is a vector field and $C$ is a piecewise-smooth, simple, closed curve, then the flux of $\\mathbf F$ across $C$ is given by\n$\\oint\\limits_C \\mathbf F \\cdot \\mathbf{n} \\: \\textrm{d}s$,\nwhere $\\mathbf{n}$ denotes the outward-pointing unit normal vector to $C$. In this case, we have\n\n$$\n\\oint\\limits_C \\mathbf F \\cdot \\mathbf{n} \\: \\textrm{d}s = \\int\\limits_{C_1} \\mathbf F \\cdot \\mathbf{n}_1 \\: \\textrm{d}s + \\int\\limits_{C_2} \\mathbf F \\cdot \\mathbf{n}_2 \\: \\textrm{d}s + \\int\\limits_{C_3} \\mathbf F \\cdot \\mathbf{n}_3 \\: \\textrm{d}s + \\int\\limits_{C_4} \\mathbf F \\cdot \\mathbf{n}_4 \\: \\textrm{d}s\n$$\n\nwhere the sides $C_i$ and unit vectors $\\mathbf n_i$ are shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/3715/Source/Images/8550241bbdc42d8ce5e3d1b6f1658d69.png)\n\nWe compute this integral in two stages.\n\n- First, consider the side $C_1$. We have that $\\mathbf n_1 = \\mathbf i$, and therefore,\n$∫_{C_{1}}F \\cdot n_{1}ds|= ∫_{C_{1}}(5xi - 3yj) \\cdot ids; = 5∫_{C_{1}}xds$.\nThis is a line integral with respect to arc length, which we can evaluate in the usual way. Parameterizing the side $C_1$, we have\n$\\mathbf r(t) = 2\\mathbf i + t\\,\\mathbf j, \\quad \\Rightarrow\\quad \\mathbf r'(t) = \\mathbf j, \\qquad t\\in [-1,1]$.\nTherefore,\n$5∫_{C_{1}}xds|= 5∫_{-1}^{1}2 \\cdot \\mid \\mid r^{′}(t) \\mid \\mid dt; = 10∫_{-1}^{1}dt; = 10 \\cdot 2; = 20$.\nBy symmetry, the flux integral across the side $C_3$ must be the same. Therefore,\n$\\int\\limits_{C_1} \\mathbf F \\cdot \\mathbf{n}_1 \\: \\textrm{d}s = \\int\\limits_{C_3} \\mathbf F \\cdot \\mathbf{n}_3 \\: \\textrm{d}s = 20$.\n\n- Next, we consider the side $C_2$. We have that $\\mathbf n_2 = \\mathbf j$, and therefore,\n$∫_{C_{2}}F \\cdot n_{2}ds|= ∫_{C_{2}}(5xi - 3yj) \\cdot jds; =-3∫_{C_{2}}yds$.\nThis is a line integral with respect to arc length, which we can evaluate in the usual way. Parameterizing the side $C_2$, we have\n$\\mathbf r(t) = t\\,\\mathbf i + \\mathbf j, \\quad \\Rightarrow\\quad \\mathbf r'(t) = \\mathbf i, \\qquad t\\in [-2,2]$.\nTherefore,\n$-3∫_{C_{2}}yds|=-3∫_{-2}^{2}1 \\cdot \\mid \\mid r^{′}(t) \\mid \\mid dt; =-3∫_{-2}^{2}dt; = (-3) \\cdot 4; =-12$.\nBy symmetry, the flux integral across the side $C_4$ must be the same. Therefore,\n$\\int\\limits_{C_2} \\mathbf F \\cdot \\mathbf{n}_2 \\: \\textrm{d}s = \\int\\limits_{C_4} \\mathbf F \\cdot \\mathbf{n}_4 \\: \\textrm{d}s = -12$.\n\nFinally, then\n\n$$\n\\oint\\limits_{C}\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathbf n\\,\\textrm d s= 2 \\cdot 20 + 2 \\cdot (-12) = 16\n$$" 13194139535290 false] [17592186179574 1020 #edb/ref 17592186341986 13194139535290 true] [17592186179574 1020 #edb/ref 17592186341993 13194139535290 true] [17592186179574 1020 #edb/ref 17592186342000 13194139535290 true] [17592186179574 1020 #edb/ref 17592186342007 13194139535290 true] [17592186179575 1020 #edb/ref 17592186342014 13194139535290 true] [17592186179575 1020 #edb/ref 17592186342021 13194139535290 true] [17592186179575 1020 #edb/ref 17592186342028 13194139535290 true] [17592186179575 1020 #edb/ref 17592186342035 13194139535290 true] [17592186179576 1020 #edb/ref 17592186341979 13194139535290 true] [17592186179576 1020 #edb/ref 17592186342042 13194139535290 true] [17592186179576 1020 #edb/ref 17592186342049 13194139535290 true] [17592186179576 1020 #edb/ref 17592186342056 13194139535290 true] [17592186179577 1020 #edb/ref 17592186342063 13194139535290 true] [17592186179577 1020 #edb/ref 17592186342070 13194139535290 true] [17592186179577 1020 #edb/ref 17592186342077 13194139535290 true] [17592186179577 1020 #edb/ref 17592186342084 13194139535290 true] [17592186179577 1020 #edb/ref 17592186342091 13194139535290 true] [17592186179963 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139535290 true] [17592186179963 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14061989/assets/772added7ce46ba0ec25de66dfd03e0107787d851006f261ac8843eba008a2d2.png)\n\nConsider the circle $S$ and vector field $F$ shown above. Let $C$ be any path where $S$ is traversed once in the counterclockwise direction. \n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\n- The flux of $F$ across $C$ is positive\n- The flux of $F$ across $C$ is zero\n- The flux of $F$ across $-C$ is negative" 13194139535290 true] [17592186179963 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/3715/Source/Images/q-126728.png)\n\nConsider the circle $S$ and vector field $F$ shown above. Let $C$ be any path where $S$ is traversed once in the counterclockwise direction.\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\n1. The flux of $F$ across $C$ is positive\n2. The flux of $F$ across $C$ is zero\n3. The flux of $F$ across $-C$ is negative" 13194139535290 false] [17592186179963 1145 "If $F$ is a vector field and $C$ is a piecewise-smooth, simple, closed curve, then the flux of $F$ across $C$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s,\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ denotes the outward-pointing unit normal vector to $C.$\n\nTo understand the flux intuitively, we imagine that $F$ represents a stream of flowing water. The flux of $F$ across $C$ represents the net flow of water escaping the region bounded by $C:$ \n\n- If more water flows out through $C$ than in through $C$ (i.e., there is a net flow *out* through $C$), the flux is *positive*.\n\n- If more water flows in through $C$ than out through $C$ (i.e., there is a net flow *in* through $C$), the flux is *negative*.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14061989/assets/a72dc9866562a42a49590fd169424307675b2c14b36833bd729026f46b677eec.png)\n\n- If the net flow through $C$ is zero then the flux equals zero.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14061989/assets/3a6ac8656b2e0b5cc482a16eb3a4fab57cb681c9a40cf634da47f9a4323d9fdf.png)\n\nFor an arbitrary closed curve, the outward-pointing unit normal vector tells us which direction is \"out.\"\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each statement.\n\n- Statement I is false, while statement II is true. From the diagram below we see that $F$ acts perpendicular to $n$ at every point on $C.$ Therefore, there is no net flow of $F$ through $C.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14061989/assets/d7a469a331a3cda238fcefb9e7163743483975c4ecc7b962d6d5f8d79736693c.png)\n\nSo, the flux of $F$ across $C$ is zero, and we must have\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s=0.\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. When measuring flux through a closed curve, the orientation of the curve does not matter because the outward-pointing unit normal vector is the same at each point on $C$ in either case.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14061989/assets/29f144d6f3ce8233a034025fe933bc57dbea5476c50a259853a98bd2a2b3a47b.png)\n\nSo, the flux of $F$ across $-C$ is zero, and we must have\n\n$$\n{∮}_{-C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s=0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\"" 13194139535290 true] [17592186179965 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139535290 true] [17592186179965 1100 "Consider the vector field $F(x, y) = x^{2}i - y^{2}j$ and the circle $C$ given by $x^{2} + y^{2} = 1$. If the flux of $F$ across $C$ is given by\n\n$∮_{C}AAAAAAAds$,\n\nwhat is the missing expression?\n\n*Hint: The outward-pointing unit normal vector to $C$ is $n = xi + yj$.*" 13194139535290 false] [17592186179965 1100 "Consider the vector field $F(x,y)={x}^{2}\\,i-{y}^{2}\\,j$ and the circle $C$ given by ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=1.$ If the flux of $F$ across $C$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}\\boxed{}\\,\\text{d}s,\n$$\n\nwhat is the missing expression?\n\n*Hint: The outward-pointing unit normal vector to $C$ is $n=x\\,i+y\\,j.$*" 13194139535290 true] [17592186179965 1145 "If $F$ is a vector field and $C$ is a piecewise-smooth, simple, closed curve, then the flux of $F$ across $C$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s,\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ denotes the outward-pointing unit normal vector to $C.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14061989/assets/386f010932b5846e7f47a605a94a5b5d580bd35dbe37512b1583d15d29f3dc70.png)\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s & ={∮}_{C}({x}^{2}\\,i-{y}^{2}\\,j)\\cdot (xi+y\\,j)\\,\\text{d}s \\\\  & ={∮}_{C}{x}^{3}-{y}^{3}\\,\\text{d}s.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the missing expression is ${x}^{3}-{y}^{3}.$" 13194139535290 true] [17592186179967 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139535290 true] [17592186179967 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14061989/assets/19fca0188077e56d6b56048d1cd48ea840922cafe7873aaa02937c8c2845e998.png)\n\nConsider the vector field $F=2\\,j$ and a square $C$ with side length $4$ centered at $O,$ as shown above. Calculate the flux of $F$ across $C.$" 13194139535290 true] [17592186179967 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/3715/Source/Images/q-126881.png)\n\nConsider the vector field $F = 2j$ and a square $C$ with side length $4$ centered at $O$, as shown above. Calculate the flux of $F$ across $C$." 13194139535290 false] [17592186179967 1145 "If $F$ is a vector field and $C$ is a piecewise-smooth, simple, closed curve, then the flux of $F$ across $C$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s,\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ denotes the outward-pointing unit normal vector to $C.$ In this case, we have\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot {n}_{1}\\,\\text{d}s+{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot {n}_{2}\\,\\text{d}s+{\\int }_{{C}_{3}}F\\cdot {n}_{3}\\,\\text{d}s+{\\int }_{{C}_{4}}F\\cdot {n}_{4}\\,\\text{d}s,\n$$\n\nwhere the sides ${C}_{i}$ and unit vectors ${n}_{i}$ are shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14061989/assets/3bd953b7fb02b90a55a29bea6f58ff9bd8bab77d62b1df1c547ca899656d37a3.png)\n\nNow, since the vector field $F=2\\,j$ is parallel to ${C}_{1}$ and ${C}_{3},$ there is zero net flow through these two sides. Therefore,\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot {n}_{1}\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{C}_{3}}F\\cdot {n}_{3}\\,\\text{d}s=0.\n$$\n\nSo, our expression for the flux reduces to\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot {n}_{2}\\,\\text{d}s+{\\int }_{{C}_{4}}F\\cdot {n}_{4}\\,\\text{d}s.\n$$\n\nNow, since the vector field is uniform, the net outflow through ${C}_{2}$ will cancel with the net inflow through ${C}_{4}.$ That is\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot {n}_{2}\\,\\text{d}s=-{\\int }_{{C}_{4}}F\\cdot {n}_{4}\\,\\text{d}s\\,⟹\\,{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot {n}_{2}\\,\\text{d}s+{\\int }_{{C}_{4}}F\\cdot {n}_{4}\\,\\text{d}s=0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s=0.\n$$" 13194139535290 true] [17592186341979 1017 #edb/ref 17592186341980 13194139535290 true] [17592186341979 1089 "q-126890" 13194139535290 true] [17592186341979 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139535290 true] [17592186341979 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14061989/assets/70a51bcda43cd04a56c6b87a10723258c995e8daae87689a2ae8af5f44112ead.png)\n\nConsider the vector field $F=x\\,i+y\\,j$ and a square $C$ with side length $4$ centered at $O,$ as shown above. Calculate the flux of $F$ across $C.$\n\n*Hint: You may use the fact that, due to symmetry, the flux is equal across all four sides of the square.*" 13194139535290 true] [17592186341979 1113 #uuid "81aec798-829c-5c5f-bf16-6135dcf124f2" 13194139535290 true] [17592186341979 1145 "If $F$ is a vector field and $C$ is a piecewise-smooth, simple, closed curve, then the flux of $F$ across $C$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s,\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ denotes the outward-pointing unit normal vector to $C.$ In this case, we have\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot {n}_{1}\\,\\text{d}s+{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot {n}_{2}\\,\\text{d}s+{\\int }_{{C}_{3}}F\\cdot {n}_{3}\\,\\text{d}s+{\\int }_{{C}_{4}}F\\cdot {n}_{4}\\,\\text{d}s,\n$$\n\nwhere the sides ${C}_{i}$ and unit vectors ${n}_{i}$ are shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14061989/assets/3bd953b7fb02b90a55a29bea6f58ff9bd8bab77d62b1df1c547ca899656d37a3.png)\n\nFirst, consider the side ${C}_{1}.$ We have that ${n}_{1}=i,$ and therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot {n}_{1}\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{{C}_{1}}(x\\,i+y\\,j)\\cdot i\\,\\text{d}s \\\\  & ={\\int }_{{C}_{1}}x\\,\\text{d}s.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is a line integral with respect to arc length, which we can evaluate in the usual way. Parameterizing the side ${C}_{1},$ we have\n\n$$\nr(t)=2\\,i+t\\,j,\\,⇒\\,{r}^{′}(t)=j,\\,t\\in [-2,2].\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{{C}_{1}}x\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{-2}^{2}2\\cdot ||{r}^{′}(t)||\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2{\\int }_{-2}^{2}\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2(2-(-2)) \\\\  & =2\\cdot 4 \\\\  & =8.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBy symmetry, the flux integrals over the sides ${C}_{2},{C}_{3},$ and ${C}_{4}$ must be the same. Therefore,\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s=4\\cdot 8=32.\n$$" 13194139535290 true] [17592186341980 1000 #edb/ref 17592186341981 13194139535290 true] [17592186341980 1000 #edb/ref 17592186341982 13194139535290 true] [17592186341980 1000 #edb/ref 17592186341983 13194139535290 true] [17592186341980 1000 #edb/ref 17592186341984 13194139535290 true] [17592186341980 1000 #edb/ref 17592186341985 13194139535290 true] [17592186341980 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139535290 true] [17592186341980 1041 "selection" 13194139535290 true] [17592186341980 1104 #edb/ref 17592186341984 13194139535290 true] [17592186341980 1110 #uuid "8970edfd-234a-5371-9d3b-9683efaf4dcd" 13194139535290 true] [17592186341981 1034 "64" 13194139535290 true] [17592186341981 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535290 true] [17592186341981 1111 #uuid "0b34799f-b5bd-5d84-8da2-db48d1f372b8" 13194139535290 true] [17592186341982 1034 "20" 13194139535290 true] [17592186341982 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535290 true] [17592186341982 1111 #uuid "c68754a5-6ba1-5fb4-80a6-a823ce91c8ac" 131941395