 true] [17592186342054 1034 "-12" 13194139535290 true] [17592186342054 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535290 true] [17592186342054 1111 #uuid "d8b461c2-022f-5193-a469-126eefc13737" 13194139535290 true] [17592186342055 1034 "-6" 13194139535290 true] [17592186342055 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535290 true] [17592186342055 1111 #uuid "0a65b489-0c15-599a-9685-17768b86c134" 13194139535290 true] [17592186342056 1017 #edb/ref 17592186342057 13194139535290 true] [17592186342056 1089 "q-127198" 13194139535290 true] [17592186342056 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139535290 true] [17592186342056 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14061989/assets/fdb05b3fcbfcfdab8344eaaaf54584a95f20696618b412873ddeb219b7a401b3.png)\n\nConsider the vector field $F=-x\\,i-y\\,j$ and a square $C$ with side length $4$ centered at $O,$ as shown above. Calculate the flux of $F$ across $C.$\n\n*Hint: You may use the fact that, due to symmetry, the flux is equal across all four sides of the square.*" 13194139535290 true] [17592186342056 1113 #uuid "50bf3cd3-875c-5457-a7a0-cb2daf3cac97" 13194139535290 true] [17592186342056 1145 "If $F$ is a vector field and $C$ is a piecewise-smooth, simple, closed curve, then the flux of $F$ across $C$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s,\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ denotes the outward-pointing unit normal vector to $C.$ In this case, we have\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot {n}_{1}\\,\\text{d}s+{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot {n}_{2}\\,\\text{d}s+{\\int }_{{C}_{3}}F\\cdot {n}_{3}\\,\\text{d}s+{\\int }_{{C}_{4}}F\\cdot {n}_{4}\\,\\text{d}s,\n$$\n\nwhere the sides ${C}_{i}$ and unit vectors ${n}_{i}$ are shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14061989/assets/3bd953b7fb02b90a55a29bea6f58ff9bd8bab77d62b1df1c547ca899656d37a3.png)\n\nFirst, consider the side ${C}_{1}.$ We have that ${n}_{1}=i,$ and therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot {n}_{1}\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{{C}_{1}}(-x\\,i-y\\,j)\\cdot i\\,\\text{d}s \\\\  & =-{\\int }_{{C}_{1}}x\\,\\text{d}s.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is a line integral with respect to arc length, which we can evaluate in the usual way. Parameterizing the side ${C}_{1},$ we have\n\n$$\nr(t)=2\\,i+t\\,j,\\,⇒\\,{r}^{′}(t)=j,\\,t\\in [-2,2].\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-{\\int }_{{C}_{1}}x\\,\\text{d}s & =-{\\int }_{-2}^{2}2\\cdot ||{r}^{′}(t)||\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =-2{\\int }_{-2}^{2}\\text{d}t \\\\  & =-2(2-(-2)) \\\\  & =(-2)\\cdot 4 \\\\  & =-8.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBy symmetry, the flux integrals over the sides ${C}_{2},{C}_{3},$ and ${C}_{4}$ must be the same. Therefore,\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s=4\\cdot (-8)=-32.\n$$" 13194139535290 true] [17592186342057 1000 #edb/ref 17592186342058 13194139535290 true] [17592186342057 1000 #edb/ref 17592186342059 13194139535290 true] [17592186342057 1000 #edb/ref 17592186342060 13194139535290 true] [17592186342057 1000 #edb/ref 17592186342061 13194139535290 true] [17592186342057 1000 #edb/ref 17592186342062 13194139535290 true] [17592186342057 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139535290 true] [17592186342057 1041 "selection" 13194139535290 true] [17592186342057 1104 #edb/ref 17592186342062 13194139535290 true] [17592186342057 1110 #uuid "98e0217d-5290-562c-bcd4-831b556096ce" 13194139535290 true] [17592186342058 1034 "-8" 13194139535290 true] [17592186342058 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535290 true] [17592186342058 1111 #uuid "2e1d4400-8cfc-57ed-a01a-9a4ed7da2c44" 13194139535290 true] [17592186342059 1034 "-16" 13194139535290 true] [17592186342059 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535290 true] [17592186342059 1111 #uuid "32e2a87a-277c-59dc-ac42-880f15b9fca1" 13194139535290 true] [17592186342060 1034 "-14" 13194139535290 true] [17592186342060 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535290 true] [17592186342060 1111 #uuid "feff3d47-b74d-5030-a612-835d56dacd65" 13194139535290 true] [17592186342061 1034 "-28" 13194139535290 true] [17592186342061 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535290 true] [17592186342061 1111 #uuid "6518b076-2b56-596f-8afe-a82e2211a888" 13194139535290 true] [17592186342062 1034 "-32" 13194139535290 true] [17592186342062 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535290 true] [17592186342062 1111 #uuid "e2e3310c-688d-5842-b878-1d9854e922a8" 13194139535290 true] [17592186342063 1017 #edb/ref 17592186342064 13194139535290 true] [17592186342063 1089 "q-127200" 13194139535290 true] [17592186342063 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139535290 true] [17592186342063 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14061989/assets/0b652e9eae16ae78a06743e75513674cc6d7596aadf2bd07aca4398a99089348.png)\n\nConsider the vector field $F=3x\\,i-y\\,j$ and a square $C$ with side length $2$ centered at $O,$ as shown above. Calculate the flux of $F$ across $C.$\n\n*Hint: You may use the fact that, due to symmetry, the flux is equal across the vertical sides, and similarly for the horizontal sides.*" 13194139535290 true] [17592186342063 1113 #uuid "ebeb72eb-38c9-5105-9bc0-9ab9b0ab0626" 13194139535290 true] [17592186342063 1145 "If $F$ is a vector field and $C$ is a piecewise-smooth, simple, closed curve, then the flux of $F$ across $C$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s,\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ denotes the outward-pointing unit normal vector to $C.$ In this case, we have\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot {n}_{1}\\,\\text{d}s+{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot {n}_{2}\\,\\text{d}s+{\\int }_{{C}_{3}}F\\cdot {n}_{3}\\,\\text{d}s+{\\int }_{{C}_{4}}F\\cdot {n}_{4}\\,\\text{d}s,\n$$\n\nwhere the sides ${C}_{i}$ and unit vectors ${n}_{i}$ are shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14061989/assets/1de4c4c55784b4a84a0493a4cc65082c430cadcc39c3d808001b2fd27665e968.png)\n\nWe compute this integral in two stages. \n\n- First, consider the side ${C}_{1}.$ We have that ${n}_{1}=i,$ and therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot {n}_{1}\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{{C}_{1}}(3x\\,i-y\\,j)\\cdot i\\,\\text{d}s \\\\  & =3{\\int }_{{C}_{1}}x\\,\\text{d}s.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is a line integral with respect to arc length, which we can evaluate in the usual way. Parameterizing the side ${C}_{1},$ we have\n\n$$\nr(t)=i+t\\,j,\\,⇒\\,{r}^{′}(t)=j,\\,t\\in [-1,1].\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3{\\int }_{{C}_{1}}x\\,\\text{d}s & =3{\\int }_{-1}^{1}1\\cdot ||{r}^{′}(t)||\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =3{\\int }_{-1}^{1}\\text{d}t \\\\  & =3\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBy symmetry, the flux integral across the side ${C}_{3}$ must be the same. Therefore,\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot {n}_{1}\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{C}_{3}}F\\cdot {n}_{3}\\,\\text{d}s=6.\n$$\n\n- Next, we consider the side ${C}_{2}.$ We have that ${n}_{2}=j,$ and therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot {n}_{2}\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{{C}_{2}}(3x\\,i-y\\,j)\\cdot j\\,\\text{d}s \\\\  & =-{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}y\\,\\text{d}s.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is a line integral with respect to arc length, which we can evaluate in the usual way. Parameterizing the side ${C}_{2},$ we have\n\n$$\nr(t)=t\\,i+j,\\,⇒\\,{r}^{′}(t)=i,\\,t\\in [-1,1].\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}y\\,\\text{d}s & =-{\\int }_{-1}^{1}1\\cdot ||{r}^{′}(t)||\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =-{\\int }_{-1}^{1}\\text{d}t \\\\  & =(-1)\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBy symmetry, the flux integral across the side ${C}_{4}$ must be the same. Therefore,\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot {n}_{2}\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{C}_{4}}F\\cdot {n}_{4}\\,\\text{d}s=-2.\n$$\n\nFinally, then\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s=2\\cdot 6+2\\cdot (-2)=8.\n$$" 13194139535290 true] [17592186342064 1000 #edb/ref 17592186342065 13194139